question_1.statement:
Sam walks on a 2×2 grid of squares labeled as follows:
\begin{tabular}[t]{|c|c|}\hline 1&2\\ \hline 4&3 \\ \hline \end{tabular}
Two squares are adjacent if they share a side. He starts at square 1 and every second walks to an adjacent square. How many different 2-step paths (i.e., visiting exactly 2 squares after the start) can Sam take?

question_2.statement:
Sam walks on a 2×2 grid of squares labeled as follows:
\begin{tabular}[t]{|c|c|}\hline 1&2\\ \hline 4&3 \\ \hline \end{tabular}
Two squares are adjacent if they share a side. He starts at square 1 and every second walks to an adjacent square. Consider all possible 3-step paths (visiting 3 squares after the start). What is the sum of the numbers on the squares visited (excluding the starting square) for the path 1 → 4 → 1 → 2?

question_3.statement:
Sam walks on a 2×2 grid of squares labeled as follows:
\begin{tabular}[t]{|c|c|}\hline 1&2\\ \hline 4&3 \\ \hline \end{tabular}
Two squares are adjacent if they share a side. He starts at square 1 and every second walks to an adjacent square. How many 4-step paths (visiting 4 squares after the start) have the sequence of visited squares summing to exactly 10?

question_4.statement:
Sam walks on a 2×2 grid of squares labeled as follows:
\begin{tabular}[t]{|c|c|}\hline 1&2\\ \hline 4&3 \\ \hline \end{tabular}
Two squares are adjacent if they share a side. He starts at the square labeled 1 and every second walks to an adjacent square. How many paths can Sam take so that the sum of the numbers on every square he visits in his path is equal to 20 (not counting the square he started on)?

